  "response": "**Calculation (Bayes' theorem):**\n\n- Prevalence: P(D) = 1/500 = 0.002, P(¬D) = 0.998\n- Sensitivity: P(+|D) = 0.98\n- False positive rate: P(+|¬D) = 1 − 0.95 = 0.05\n\nP(+) = (0.002 × 0.98) + (0.998 × 0.05) = 0.00196 + 0.0499 = 0.05186\n\nP(D|+) = 0.00196 / 0.05186 ≈ 0.037794 = 3.7794%\n\nRounded to two decimal places: **3.78%**\n\nSo even with a positive test result, there's only about a 3.78% chance the person actually has the condition — a classic illustration of how low base rates dominate posterior probability even with a fairly accurate test.\n\nFINAL ANSWER: 3.78",
